#########################################################################
# ==============================================================================
# THE KEYENCE HAS TO BE IN ITS MEASURING WINDOW, AND SOMETHING HAS TO CHECK
# ==============================================================================
# The LJ-V7080 measures a surface only from 0.057 to 0.103 m from its head -- the
# 80 mm reference distance plus or minus 23 mm. Noether plans the Keyence raster
# OVER A ROUGH MESH, with the optical frame placed on that surface, so an error
# in the mesh becomes an error in the commanded standoff, one for one.
#
# At the bottom of a triangulation profiler's range the return geometry degrades
# badly and the sensor does NOT fail cleanly: it returns wrong values, which are
# then meshed, Poisson-reconstructed, decimated and handed to the eddy-current
# planner as a surface. Every stage downstream reports success.
#
# THE FAILURE THE CONSTANTS BELOW ARE SIZED AGAINST. A rough mesh from a
# miscalibrated depth camera read a flat surface-ground plate 13.6 mm low across
# the part and about 22 mm low at one end. The raster commanded 80 mm everywhere,
# so the sensor flew at 66 mm over most of the plate -- inside the window, 9 mm
# above the near limit, which is why most of it worked -- and at 58-61 mm over
# the bad end, one to four millimetres from the hard limit. Between 26% and 39%
# of the plate came back as points scattered over a 16 mm range instead of a flat
# surface, and nothing in the pipeline noticed at any stage.
#
# Recovered from the raw profiles of that raster, per 16 mm band across the
# plate, the signature is unambiguous:
#
#     standoff median   p5..p95 spread   within 5 mm of the limit
#         64.6 mm           19.2 mm             41.7%
#         66.3 mm           17.6 mm              0.0%
#         66.8 mm           17.1 mm              0.0%
#         66.3 mm           17.0 mm              0.0%
#         66.2 mm            7.5 mm              0.0%
#      66.4..68.5 mm    0.20..0.26 mm            0.0%
#
# A surface-ground plate is flat to a fraction of a millimetre, so the 0.2 mm
# rows are the sensor working and the 17-19 mm rows are it failing.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE CHECKS, IN THREE LAYERS, AND WHY IT TAKES THREE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No single check catches this. They are listed in the order they run.
#
# 1. THE PLAN-TIME GATE -- keyence_standoff_gate(), called from
#    _KeyenceReconstruction.plan_sweep(). Takes the planned probe_end poses and
#    a surface, casts the sensor's own optical axis from every waypoint, and
#    measures how far the first surface is. REFUSES a plan that leaves less than
#    the margin inside the window.
#
#    Against the mesh the raster was planned over this is a CONSISTENCY check
#    and it should read 80 mm everywhere, because that is what the plan asked
#    for. It is worth running anyway, because "everywhere" is where it stops
#    being true: a pose re-oriented by the clearance gate after the sensor
#    offset was applied, a z_offset that is not zero, a wrong keyence_ref_dist,
#    a waypoint aimed across a hole so that the ray hits nothing at all, and a
#    vertical tool over a curved part whose ray grazes a nearer ridge than the
#    waypoint it was planned for, all show up here and nowhere else.
#
#    Given a BETTER surface -- surface=, a truth mesh, a plane from a touch-off,
#    the Keyence's own mesh from a previous run -- the same call grades the plan
#    against that instead, and a raster planned over a mesh that is wrong by more
#    than the margin is refused outright. That is what keyence_standoff_test.py
#    asserts.
#
# 2. THE PRE-FLIGHT GATE -- keyence_range_gate(), called from
#    _KeyenceReconstruction.profile_facts(), which runs after the arm has
#    reached the first pose and before a single row is flown. The sensor is
#    already looking at the part, so it can be asked what it SEES, and that
#    answer is compared against what the plan COMMANDED at the same waypoint.
#    The difference is the error in the rough mesh, measured, in seconds,
#    with no touch-off and no calibration.
#
#    On the failure above it reads: commanded 80.0 mm, measured 66.4 mm, so the
#    rough mesh is 13.6 mm low here. That is over KEYENCE_MESH_BIAS_LIMIT_M and
#    the sweep is refused before the ten minutes are spent. See that constant for
#    why 10 mm rather than "is it in the window" -- 66 mm IS in the window, and
#    a guard that only asks that question passes a raster like that one.
#
# 3. THE IN-FLIGHT MONITOR -- KeyenceStandoffMonitor, subscribed to /profiles
#    for the duration of the sweep and reporting per pass through progress.dpi.
#    This is the only layer that sees a LOCAL mesh error: a mesh can be 13.6 mm
#    wrong across a part and 22 mm wrong at one end, and it is the end that
#    produces garbage. The monitor says so on the pass it happens, with the
#    fraction of that pass's points inside the margin, rather than in a mesh
#    somebody looks at the next day.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO TOUCH-OFF. Nothing in this file may require one: a contact touch-off is how
# the cell is CALIBRATED, and it is not part of the automated pipeline. Layer 1
# uses the planned geometry, layer 2 and layer 3 use the sensor's own returns. A
# touch-off can be handed in as `surface=` when one exists, and that is all.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE GEOMETRY, WRITTEN DOWN ONCE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read off tari_ur_description/urdf/lj_v7080_macro.xacro and confirmed by
# chaining the rendered URDF:
#
#   lj_v7080_frame              the head. The measuring window is measured from
#                               here: 0.057 to 0.103 m.
#   lj_v7080_optical_frame      at (-0.0022, -0.0005, -keyence_ref_dist) in the
#                               head's frame, i.e. 80 mm out along the head's
#                               -z. It is the plane a raw reading of zero means,
#                               which is why the raster is planned with THIS
#                               frame on the surface.
#   probe_end -> optical        a pure Rz(+90 deg) with translation
#                               (-0.07515, -0.0022, -0.03428) on the ec tooling.
#                               The optical frame's z axis is probe_end's z
#                               axis, so the sensor looks along probe_end's -z,
#                               which is the direction Noether's poses already
#                               point at the surface.
#
# So, with `t` measured along the look direction from the optical frame origin,
# positive away from the head:
#
#     standoff = keyence_ref_dist + t
#
# and the profiler's own reported z, which keyence_driver_node packs as
# (x_across, 0, z) in lj_v7080_optical_frame with POSITIVE z TOWARDS THE HEAD
# (tari_keyence_sim's z_sign, and the device's convention):
#
#     standoff = keyence_ref_dist - z
#
# z = +0.023 is the near limit, 0.057 m; z = -0.023 is the far limit, 0.103 m.
# Both expressions are the same quantity from the two ends of the pipeline,
# which is exactly what layer 2 subtracts.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Numpy only. No ROS for anything but the live subscription, no open3d, no
# service: a guard that needs the cell up is a guard that cannot refuse before
# the cell is up. Python 3.10 -- the work cell runs ROS natively on Ubuntu
# 22.04 and has no container to hide a newer interpreter in.

import os
import math
import time
import threading

import numpy as np

# A .dpi is compiled as a FUNCTION BODY sharing one globals() dict with every
# other .dpi, so a top-level def or assignment binds a LOCAL of the include
# unless it is declared global first -- invisible to every other module AND to
# the functions in this one. dpi_exports_test.py refuses the pattern; everything
# defined below is listed here.
global KEYENCE_RANGE_M
global KEYENCE_REF_DIST_M
global KEYENCE_STANDOFF_MARGIN_M
global KEYENCE_MESH_BIAS_LIMIT_M
global KEYENCE_LINE_FLAT_SPREAD_M
global KEYENCE_STANDOFF_WARN_FRACTION
global KEYENCE_RAY_SEARCH_M
global KEYENCE_MONITOR_TOPIC
global keyence_window
global keyence_standoff_margin
global keyence_ref_dist
global keyence_optical_geometry
global commanded_standoff
global keyence_standoff_report
global describe_standoff_report
global keyence_standoff_gate
global check_keyence_standoff
global keyence_profile_standoff
global keyence_range_report
global describe_range_report
global keyence_range_gate
global KeyenceStandoffMonitor
global last_standoff_report
global last_range_report
global _standoff_say
global _surface_geometry
global _describe_surface
global _ray_first_hit
global _tri_grid
global _fmt_mm
global _pass_of
global _worst_side
global _standoff_of_path
global out_of_window_shape


#: The LJ-V7080's measuring window, metres from the head (lj_v7080_frame /
#: laser_link). The reference distance plus and minus 23 mm, from the head's
#: specification; it is also the range the simulated profiler enforces and the
#: range the old Gazebo gpu_ray was configured with, so simulation and hardware
#: refuse in the same place.
KEYENCE_RANGE_M = (0.057, 0.103)

#: Where in that window the optical frame sits -- the plane a raw reading of
#: zero means, and therefore the standoff the raster commands when it puts that
#: frame on the surface. It is `keyence_ref_dist` in lj_v7080_macro.xacro, and a
#: value that disagrees with the URDF's puts every profile low in world by the
#: difference. A cell that has measured `keyence_ref_dist_cal` should set
#: KEYENCE_REF_DIST in its config to the same total, so this file and the URDF
#: agree.
KEYENCE_REF_DIST_M = 0.080

#: How much of the window a plan must leave unused at EACH end: the guarded
#: window is 0.062..0.098 m.
#:
#: 5 mm, and it is not arbitrary. It is the same number tooling_clearance.dpi
#: uses for the same reason -- the rough mesh a raster is planned over is a 6 mm
#: voxel TSDF, so a few millimetres IS the resolution of the surface the plan is
#: made from, and a plan that sits closer than that to a limit is not inside the
#: window, it is inside the noise. For scale, a raster measured at 66.4 mm leaves
#: 4.4 mm of this margin unspent; one at 60.8 mm is 1.2 mm past it.
#:
#: Override per cell with KEYENCE_STANDOFF_MARGIN in the config. Raising it is a
#: statement about how much you distrust the planning surface. LOWERING it is
#: not a fix for a refusal -- see the refusal text.
KEYENCE_STANDOFF_MARGIN_M = 0.005

#: How far the surface a raster was planned over may sit from the surface the
#: sensor actually reports, before the plan stops being worth flying. Metres.
#:
#: 10 mm. The window is 46 mm wide; after the margin above, 18 mm of it is usable
#: either side of the reference distance. A planning surface that is wrong by
#: more than half of that has spent the budget GLOBALLY, and whatever made it
#: wrong -- a loose camera mount, a bad calibration -- has no reason to be wrong
#: by the same amount everywhere: 13.6 mm across a plate and 22 mm at one end is
#: the measured shape of it, and the end is what came back as garbage. So the
#: question the pre-flight gate asks is not "is the sensor in its window here",
#: which at 66 mm is honestly yes, but "is the surface this raster was planned
#: over good enough that the rest of it will be".
KEYENCE_MESH_BIAS_LIMIT_M = 0.010

#: How much height the laser line may span before the surface under it counts as
#: CURVED ACROSS THE LINE rather than flat, metres.
#:
#: 3 mm. A flat plate measures 0.3-1.9 mm of p5..p95 spread in simulation and
#: 0.10 mm on a sprayed flat at the cell, so 3 mm is well clear of a flat surface
#: and well under what a real curve gives: a 131 x 65 x 67 mm cylinder spans tens
#: of millimetres across the same 31 mm line.
#:
#: What it gates is the INFERENCE, not the measurement -- see the note in
#: keyence_range_report() where the bias is computed. Over this, "the median
#: disagrees with the commanded standoff" stops being evidence about the mesh,
#: because the plan commands a standoff at the MIDDLE of the line and a curved
#: surface puts that point at one END of the returned range.
KEYENCE_LINE_FLAT_SPREAD_M = 0.003

#: Fraction of one pass's returns inside the margin that makes the pass worth
#: shouting about. 2%: in the failure above the worst pass was 41.7% and every
#: good pass 0.0%, so anything in this range separates them. It is low on purpose
#: -- a pass with 2% of its points at the limit has a bad patch in it, and a bad
#: patch is what gets meshed.
KEYENCE_STANDOFF_WARN_FRACTION = 0.02

#: How far either side of the reference distance the plan-time ray is searched,
#: metres. Wider than the window so a plan that is badly out can be REPORTED as
#: badly out rather than as "no surface found", which is a different fault.
KEYENCE_RAY_SEARCH_M = 0.060

#: Where the profiler publishes. Same default as
#: _KeyenceReconstruction.PROFILE_TOPIC, read through the source when there is
#: one so a cell that remaps it only says so once.
KEYENCE_MONITOR_TOPIC = "/profiles"

#: The last report each layer produced, for a caller who wants the numbers
#: rather than the printed page.
last_standoff_report = None
last_range_report = None


def _standoff_say(text):
    """One line, through progress.dpi when it is loaded and print() otherwise.

    progress_say() reaches the operator from a worker thread, which a bare
    print() does not -- see the header of progress.dpi. Not every config that
    can fly a Keyence raster includes it, though, so this must not be the thing
    that raises when it is missing.
    """
    say = globals().get("progress_note")
    if say is not None:
        try:
            say(text)
            return
        except Exception:
            pass
    print(text, flush=True)


def _fmt_mm(v):
    """A standoff in millimetres, or a word when there is no number."""
    if v is None:
        return "none"
    v = float(v)
    if not np.isfinite(v):
        return "nothing in range"
    return "%.1f mm" % (1e3*v,)


def keyence_standoff_margin(margin=None):
    """How much of the window to leave unused at each end, metres.

    Explicit argument, then the config's KEYENCE_STANDOFF_MARGIN, then this
    module's KEYENCE_STANDOFF_MARGIN_M. The same three-step resolution every
    other number here uses, so a cell overrides in one place and every layer
    picks it up.
    """
    if margin is not None:
        return float(margin)
    cfg = globals().get("KEYENCE_STANDOFF_MARGIN")
    if cfg is not None:
        return float(cfg)
    return float(KEYENCE_STANDOFF_MARGIN_M)


def keyence_ref_dist(ref_dist=None):
    """The reference distance the optical frame sits at, metres.

    KEYENCE_REF_DIST in the config is `keyence_ref_dist + keyence_ref_dist_cal`
    from the cell's URDF. They are two settings in two trees and only one of
    them moves the robot, which is the same shape of trap as the Azure's depth
    mode -- so a cell that has calibrated one should set the other.
    """
    if ref_dist is not None:
        return float(ref_dist)
    cfg = globals().get("KEYENCE_REF_DIST")
    if cfg is not None:
        return float(cfg)
    return float(KEYENCE_REF_DIST_M)


def keyence_window(margin=None, range_m=None):
    """(guarded_lo, guarded_hi, hard_lo, hard_hi) in metres.

    The hard window is the sensor's; the guarded one is what a plan has to stay
    inside. Everything in this file compares against the guarded pair and
    reports both, because "1 mm inside the specification" and "4 mm inside the
    guard" are different sentences and the operator needs to be told which.
    """
    hard = tuple(float(v) for v in
                 (range_m if range_m is not None
                  else globals().get("KEYENCE_RANGE") or KEYENCE_RANGE_M))
    m = keyence_standoff_margin(margin)
    lo, hi = hard[0] + m, hard[1] - m
    if lo >= hi:
        raise ValueError(
            "a standoff margin of %.1f mm leaves nothing of the LJ-V7080's "
            "%.0f..%.0f mm window. The window is %.0f mm wide; the margin is "
            "per end." % (1e3*m, 1e3*hard[0], 1e3*hard[1],
                          1e3*(hard[1] - hard[0])))
    return lo, hi, hard[0], hard[1]


# ==============================================================================
# LAYER 1: WHAT THE PLAN COMMANDS
# ==============================================================================

def keyence_optical_geometry(tf_offset=None, axis=None, ref_dist=None,
                             source=None):
    """(offset, axis, ref_dist): probe_end -> the sensor, from live TF.

    `offset` is probe_end -> lj_v7080_optical_frame in the PROBE_END frame, the
    same vector _KeyenceReconstruction.keyence_offset() returns and the one
    plan_sweep() moves every pose back along. `axis` is the unit look direction
    in the probe_end frame -- from the head towards the surface.

    THE AXIS IS READ, NOT ASSUMED, when TF has it. ros.keyence_t carries the
    full transform, and the optical frame's -z is the measuring axis (the head
    sits at +keyence_ref_dist along its +z). On the ec tooling that rotation is
    a pure Rz(+90 deg), so the axis comes out as probe_end's -z exactly -- which
    is the default when there is no transform and the reason a cell whose sensor
    is mounted square can be checked with nothing running. A cell that tilts the
    head gets the tilt for free; a cell that tilts the head and has no TF gets
    the default and is told so by keyence_offset()'s own refusal long before
    this is reached.

    `source` is a _KeyenceReconstruction, if the caller has one: its
    keyence_offset() is preferred over ros.keyence_t so an explicit tf_offset=
    passed to the constructor is honoured here too.
    """
    ref = keyence_ref_dist(ref_dist)
    ax = None if axis is None else np.asarray(axis, dtype=np.float64).reshape(3)
    off = None if tf_offset is None else np.asarray(tf_offset,
                                                    dtype=np.float64).reshape(3)
    if off is None and source is not None:
        off = np.asarray(source.keyence_offset(), dtype=np.float64).reshape(3)
    t = getattr(globals().get("ros"), "keyence_t", None)
    if off is None:
        if t is None:
            raise RuntimeError(
                "no probe_end -> lj_v7080_optical_frame transform (ros.keyence_t "
                "is None), so where the sensor is relative to the poses is "
                "unknown and the standoff cannot be checked. Pass "
                "tf_offset=(dx, dy, dz), or check_keyence_standoff(source=) a "
                "reconstruction that has one.")
        off = np.array([t.transform.translation.x, t.transform.translation.y,
                        t.transform.translation.z], dtype=np.float64)
    if ax is None and t is not None:
        r = t.transform.rotation
        q = np.array([r.x, r.y, r.z, r.w], dtype=np.float64)
        if np.isfinite(q).all() and np.linalg.norm(q) > 1e-9:
            # -z of the optical frame, expressed in probe_end.
            ax = -quat_to_matrix(q / np.linalg.norm(q))[:, 2]
    if ax is None:
        ax = np.array([0.0, 0.0, -1.0])
    n = float(np.linalg.norm(ax))
    if n < 1e-9:
        raise ValueError("the Keyence optical axis is degenerate: %r" % (ax,))
    return off, ax / n, ref


def _surface_geometry(surface):
    """Anything the guard accepts as a surface -> ("mesh", v, tris) or
    ("plane", z, None).

    Accepted, in the order they are tried:

      * a float, or {"plane_z": z}          a horizontal plane at that world z.
        This is how a contact touch-off is handed in on the rare occasion one
        exists, and how a test states that the truth is flat at a known height.
      * a path to a PLY                     read with noether_path.dpi's
        read_ply_mesh, which is the same reader the planner's own gate uses.
      * (vertices, triangles)               already in memory.
      * (vertices, normals, triangles)      read_ply_mesh's own tuple, passed
                                            straight back in.

    A POINT CLOUD IS REFUSED. A raw Keyence cloud is millions of points and no
    faces; casting a ray at it finds nothing, and "nothing in range" is the
    report this guard gives for a sensor aimed at a hole. Confusing the two
    would turn a wrong filename into a fake refusal, so say which it is.
    """
    if surface is None:
        return None
    if isinstance(surface, (int, float)) and not isinstance(surface, bool):
        return ("plane", float(surface), None)
    if isinstance(surface, dict):
        if "plane_z" in surface:
            return ("plane", float(surface["plane_z"]), None)
        raise ValueError("a surface dict must carry plane_z, not %s"
                         % (sorted(surface),))
    if isinstance(surface, str):
        path = os.path.abspath(surface)
        if not os.path.exists(path):
            raise IOError("no surface to check the standoff against: %s" % (path,))
        # ASKED OF THE HEADER, before the reader is handed the file.
        # read_ply_mesh() refuses a faceless PLY with "has no vertices or no
        # faces", which is true and is the wrong sentence here: what the caller
        # did was point this at one of the SIX point clouds the Keyence chain
        # writes, and the useful answer names them.
        nface, nvert = None, None
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            head = f.read(4096).split(b"end_header")[0]
        for line in head.decode("ascii", "replace").splitlines():
            w = line.split()
            if len(w) >= 3 and w[0] == "element" and w[1] == "face":
                nface = int(w[2])
            elif len(w) >= 3 and w[0] == "element" and w[1] == "vertex":
                nvert = int(w[2])
        if nface in (None, 0):
            raise ValueError(
                "%s is a POINT CLOUD -- %s vertices and no faces -- so there is "
                "no surface for the sensor's axis to intersect. The Keyence "
                "chain writes six of these (_raw, _merged, _cluster, _cropped, "
                "_sor_filtered, _final_points); the meshes are _ball_pivot, "
                "_poisson and _qudric_decimation."
                % (path, "no" if nvert is None else nvert))
        v, _n, tris = read_ply_mesh(path)
        return ("mesh", np.asarray(v, dtype=np.float64),
                np.asarray(tris, dtype=np.int64))
    seq = list(surface)
    if len(seq) == 3 and seq[1] is not None and np.asarray(seq[1]).ndim == 2 \
            and np.asarray(seq[1]).shape == np.asarray(seq[0]).shape:
        seq = [seq[0], seq[2]]              # (verts, normals, tris)
    elif len(seq) == 3:
        seq = [seq[0], seq[2]]
    if len(seq) != 2:
        raise ValueError("a surface is a plane z, a PLY path, (verts, tris) or "
                         "(verts, normals, tris); got %r" % (surface,))
    return ("mesh", np.asarray(seq[0], dtype=np.float64),
            np.asarray(seq[1], dtype=np.int64))


def _describe_surface(surface, geom=None):
    """What the report calls the thing it measured against."""
    if surface is None:
        return "nothing"
    if isinstance(surface, str):
        return os.path.basename(surface)
    if geom is not None and geom[0] == "plane":
        return "a horizontal plane at z %.4f" % (geom[1],)
    if geom is not None:
        return "a mesh of %d faces" % (len(geom[2]),)
    return repr(surface)


def _tri_grid(verts, tris, cell):
    """A uniform xy bucket grid over the triangles: (x0, y0, cell, keys, order,
    starts).

    Broad phase, and it is what makes this affordable. The narrow phase is
    Moller-Trumbore, which costs one pass over every candidate triangle per ray;
    a 30 000-face rough mesh times 4 000 waypoints is 1.2e8 of those, which is
    minutes and gigabytes of temporaries. The ray only has to be searched over
    about 120 mm, so its xy footprint covers a handful of cells and the
    candidate set drops to tens.

    Built with np.repeat and one argsort rather than a dict, because a Python
    loop over the faces of a Poisson mesh is itself several seconds.
    """
    lo = np.minimum(np.minimum(verts[tris[:, 0]], verts[tris[:, 1]]),
                    verts[tris[:, 2]])
    hi = np.maximum(np.maximum(verts[tris[:, 0]], verts[tris[:, 1]]),
                    verts[tris[:, 2]])
    x0, y0 = float(lo[:, 0].min()), float(lo[:, 1].min())
    # A CAP ON THE GRID, not on the candidates. The cell above is sized from the
    # MEDIAN face, and a reconstructed mesh is not uniform: ball pivoting leaves
    # slivers and Poisson leaves a few faces spanning a hole, so one triangle
    # can cover the whole grid and the (cell, triangle) list can blow up while
    # the median says everything is fine. Widening the cell until the list fits
    # keeps the answer exactly right -- a coarser grid means more candidates per
    # ray, never a missed one -- where clipping a triangle's span would lose
    # surface and silently under-report the standoff.
    for _round in range(12):
        nx = max(1, int(math.ceil((float(hi[:, 0].max()) - x0)/cell)) + 1)
        ny = max(1, int(math.ceil((float(hi[:, 1].max()) - y0)/cell)) + 1)
        ix0 = np.clip(((lo[:, 0] - x0)/cell).astype(np.int64), 0, nx - 1)
        ix1 = np.clip(((hi[:, 0] - x0)/cell).astype(np.int64), 0, nx - 1)
        iy0 = np.clip(((lo[:, 1] - y0)/cell).astype(np.int64), 0, ny - 1)
        iy1 = np.clip(((hi[:, 1] - y0)/cell).astype(np.int64), 0, ny - 1)
        wx, wy = ix1 - ix0 + 1, iy1 - iy0 + 1
        counts = wx*wy
        if int(counts.sum()) <= 8_000_000 and nx*ny <= 4_000_000:
            break
        cell *= 2.0
    tri_idx = np.repeat(np.arange(len(tris), dtype=np.int64), counts)
    # Position of each entry within its own triangle's (wx, wy) block.
    within = np.arange(int(counts.sum()), dtype=np.int64) - np.repeat(
        np.concatenate(([0], np.cumsum(counts)[:-1])), counts)
    wxr = np.repeat(wx, counts)
    cx = np.repeat(ix0, counts) + (within % wxr)
    cy = np.repeat(iy0, counts) + (within // wxr)
    keys = cy*nx + cx
    order = np.argsort(keys, kind="stable")
    keys = keys[order]
    tri_idx = tri_idx[order]
    # CSR: starts[k] .. starts[k+1] are the entries of cell k.
    starts = np.searchsorted(keys, np.arange(nx*ny + 1, dtype=np.int64))
    return (x0, y0, float(cell), nx, ny, tri_idx, starts)


def _ray_first_hit(origins, dirs, geom, search=None, progress_what=None):
    """(n,) signed distance from each origin, along its direction, to the first
    surface; +inf where the ray finds nothing within +/- `search`.

    NEGATIVE means the surface is between the origin and the head -- nearer than
    the optical frame, which is the failure mode this file exists for. The
    returned value is `t`, and the standoff is keyence_ref_dist + t.

    "First" is the SMALLEST t, not the smallest |t|: the sensor sees the nearest
    surface along its axis starting from the head, and on a curved part with a
    vertical tool that is not always the waypoint the pose was planned for.
    """
    search = float(KEYENCE_RAY_SEARCH_M if search is None else search)
    origins = np.asarray(origins, dtype=np.float64).reshape(-1, 3)
    dirs = np.asarray(dirs, dtype=np.float64).reshape(-1, 3)
    n = origins.shape[0]
    out = np.full(n, np.inf)
    if geom is None or n == 0:
        return out

    if geom[0] == "plane":
        z = geom[1]
        dz = dirs[:, 2]
        with np.errstate(divide="ignore", invalid="ignore"):
            t = np.where(np.abs(dz) > 1e-12, (z - origins[:, 2])/dz, np.inf)
        return np.where(np.abs(t) <= search, t, np.inf)

    verts, tris = geom[1], geom[2]
    v0 = verts[tris[:, 0]]
    e1 = verts[tris[:, 1]] - v0
    e2 = verts[tris[:, 2]] - v0
    # THE CELL IS SIZED FOR THE TRIANGLES, NOT FOR THE RAY. Sizing it to 2*search
    # makes the ray's footprint fit in one cell, which is true and useless: it
    # also makes the whole mesh one bucket, so every ray is tested against every
    # face, and cost then grows with mesh size. Sizing the grid to the faces
    # instead -- about sqrt(n) cells a side, never finer than a face -- holds a
    # 4600-pose raster to a fraction of a second against both a 31k-face TSDF
    # mesh and a 199k-face Poisson mesh, so the bigger mesh is not the slower one.
    #
    # A near-vertical ray still covers exactly one cell, because its footprint
    # comes from its own direction below, not from `search`.
    span = np.linalg.norm(np.maximum(np.abs(e1), np.abs(e2)), axis=1)
    # max - min written out: ndarray.ptp() was removed in numpy 2 and the host
    # is on it while the cell is not, so the one spelling that works on both is
    # the arithmetic.
    extent = max(float(verts[:, 0].max() - verts[:, 0].min()),
                 float(verts[:, 1].max() - verts[:, 1].min()), 1e-6)
    cell = max(float(np.median(span))*2.0, extent/max(math.sqrt(len(tris)), 1.0),
               1e-4)
    x0, y0, cell, nx, ny, tri_idx, starts = _tri_grid(verts, tris, cell)

    p0 = origins - search*dirs
    p1 = origins + search*dirs
    lox = np.minimum(p0[:, 0], p1[:, 0])
    hix = np.maximum(p0[:, 0], p1[:, 0])
    loy = np.minimum(p0[:, 1], p1[:, 1])
    hiy = np.maximum(p0[:, 1], p1[:, 1])
    ix0 = np.clip(((lox - x0)/cell).astype(np.int64), 0, nx - 1)
    ix1 = np.clip(((hix - x0)/cell).astype(np.int64), 0, nx - 1)
    iy0 = np.clip(((loy - y0)/cell).astype(np.int64), 0, ny - 1)
    iy1 = np.clip(((hiy - y0)/cell).astype(np.int64), 0, ny - 1)

    prog = globals().get("progress")
    ctx = (prog(progress_what, total=n, unit="waypoints")
           if (prog is not None and progress_what) else None)
    if ctx is not None:
        ctx.start()
    try:
        for i in range(n):
            cand = []
            for cy in range(iy0[i], iy1[i] + 1):
                base = cy*nx
                a = starts[base + ix0[i]]
                b = starts[base + ix1[i] + 1]
                if b > a:
                    cand.append(tri_idx[a:b])
            if ctx is not None:
                ctx.step()
            if not cand:
                continue
            c = cand[0] if len(cand) == 1 else np.concatenate(cand)
            d = dirs[i]
            a0, a1, a2 = v0[c], e1[c], e2[c]
            pv = np.cross(np.broadcast_to(d, a2.shape), a2)
            det = np.einsum("ij,ij->i", a1, pv)
            ok = np.abs(det) > 1e-14
            if not ok.any():
                continue
            inv = np.where(ok, 1.0/np.where(ok, det, 1.0), 0.0)
            tv = origins[i] - a0
            u = np.einsum("ij,ij->i", tv, pv)*inv
            qv = np.cross(tv, a1)
            v = (qv @ d)*inv
            t = np.einsum("ij,ij->i", a2, qv)*inv
            ok &= (u >= -1e-9) & (v >= -1e-9) & (u + v <= 1.0 + 1e-9)
            ok &= np.abs(t) <= search
            if ok.any():
                out[i] = float(t[ok].min())
    finally:
        if ctx is not None:
            ctx.finish()
    return out


def _standoff_of_path(path, surface, tf_offset=None, axis=None, ref_dist=None,
                      source=None, search=None, quiet=True):
    """(standoff (n,), optical origins (n,3), axis directions (n,3))."""
    off, ax, ref = keyence_optical_geometry(tf_offset=tf_offset, axis=axis,
                                            ref_dist=ref_dist, source=source)
    pos = np.asarray(path.positions, dtype=np.float64).reshape(-1, 3)
    quats = np.asarray(path.quats, dtype=np.float64).reshape(-1, 4)
    n = pos.shape[0]
    optical = np.empty((n, 3))
    look = np.empty((n, 3))
    for i in range(n):
        R = quat_to_matrix(quats[i])
        optical[i] = pos[i] + R.dot(off)
        look[i] = R.dot(ax)
    geom = _surface_geometry(surface)
    t = _ray_first_hit(optical, look, geom, search=search,
                       progress_what=None if quiet else
                       "checking the keyence standoff")
    return ref + t, optical, look


def commanded_standoff(path, surface, **kwargs):
    """(n,) commanded standoff in metres, +inf where the sensor is aimed at
    nothing within the search band.

    "Commanded" because it is what the PLAN asks the sensor to measure at: the
    distance from the head, along the head's own axis, to the first surface the
    ray meets. Over the mesh the raster was planned over it should be the
    reference distance everywhere, because plan_sweep() puts the optical frame
    on that surface and the optical frame is at the reference distance. Where it
    is not, something has moved the pose since -- or the ray is finding a nearer
    piece of the part than the waypoint it was planned for.
    """
    return _standoff_of_path(path, surface, **kwargs)[0]


def _pass_of(path, index):
    """Which segment of `path` a waypoint index belongs to, 0-based."""
    for k, (a, b) in enumerate(path.segments):
        if a <= index < b:
            return k
    return -1


def _worst_side(value, lo, hi):
    """"near", "far" or None for where `value` leaves the window."""
    if not np.isfinite(value):
        return "missing"
    if value < lo:
        return "near"
    if value > hi:
        return "far"
    return None


def keyence_standoff_report(path, surface, margin=None, range_m=None,
                            quiet=True, **kwargs):
    """What the plan commands, measured against the window. Moves nothing.

    Returns a dict:

      ok              every waypoint inside the guarded window
      standoff        (n,) metres, +inf where the ray found no surface
      lo, hi          the guarded window
      hard_lo, hard_hi  the sensor's own
      margin          how much of the window is being held back, per end
      n, n_out, n_missing
      worst           the standoff furthest outside the guarded window
      worst_index, worst_xyz, worst_pass, worst_side
      passes          one dict per segment, in order
      passes_out      indices of the segments with a waypoint out
      surface         what it was measured against, for the printed line
    """
    lo, hi, hard_lo, hard_hi = keyence_window(margin=margin, range_m=range_m)
    d, optical, _look = _standoff_of_path(path, surface, quiet=quiet, **kwargs)
    missing = ~np.isfinite(d)
    out = (d < lo) | (d > hi) | missing
    # How far outside, with a miss counted as maximally outside so it sorts to
    # the top: a waypoint whose sensor is aimed at nothing measures nothing, and
    # that is not better news than one aimed 2 mm too close.
    excess = np.where(missing, np.inf,
                      np.maximum(lo - np.where(missing, lo, d),
                                 np.where(missing, hi, d) - hi))
    passes = []
    for k, (a, b) in enumerate(path.segments):
        seg = d[a:b]
        segout = out[a:b]
        finite = seg[np.isfinite(seg)]
        passes.append({
            "index": k, "first": int(a), "last": int(b) - 1, "n": int(b - a),
            "n_out": int(segout.sum()),
            "n_missing": int((~np.isfinite(seg)).sum()),
            "min": float(finite.min()) if len(finite) else float("inf"),
            "max": float(finite.max()) if len(finite) else float("inf"),
            "median": float(np.median(finite)) if len(finite) else float("inf"),
        })
    worst_i = int(np.argmax(excess)) if len(excess) else -1
    worst = (float(d[worst_i]) if worst_i >= 0 else float("nan"))
    geom = _surface_geometry(surface)
    finite = d[np.isfinite(d)]
    rep = {
        "ok": not bool(out.any()),
        "standoff": d, "optical": optical,
        "lo": lo, "hi": hi, "hard_lo": hard_lo, "hard_hi": hard_hi,
        "margin": keyence_standoff_margin(margin),
        "ref_dist": keyence_ref_dist(kwargs.get("ref_dist")),
        "n": int(len(d)), "n_out": int(out.sum()),
        "n_missing": int(missing.sum()),
        "min": float(finite.min()) if len(finite) else float("inf"),
        "max": float(finite.max()) if len(finite) else float("inf"),
        "median": float(np.median(finite)) if len(finite) else float("inf"),
        "worst": worst, "worst_index": worst_i,
        "worst_xyz": (optical[worst_i].copy() if worst_i >= 0
                      else np.zeros(3)),
        "worst_pass": _pass_of(path, worst_i) if worst_i >= 0 else -1,
        "worst_side": _worst_side(worst, lo, hi) if worst_i >= 0 else None,
        "passes": passes,
        "out": out, "missing": missing,
        "passes_out": [p["index"] for p in passes if p["n_out"]],
        "surface": _describe_surface(surface, geom),
        "n_passes": len(passes),
    }
    return rep


def describe_standoff_report(rep, what="keyence sweep"):
    """The report as lines. The refusal and the check print the SAME text.

    Four things, in this order: how bad, where, which end of the window, and
    what to do about it.
    """
    lines = []
    lines.append(
        "    commanded standoff over %s: %s..%s (median %s); the LJ-V7080 "
        "measures from %.0f to %.0f mm and this guard holds %.0f..%.0f "
        "(%.0f mm margin)"
        % (rep["surface"], _fmt_mm(rep["min"]), _fmt_mm(rep["max"]),
           _fmt_mm(rep["median"]), 1e3*rep["hard_lo"], 1e3*rep["hard_hi"],
           1e3*rep["lo"], 1e3*rep["hi"], 1e3*rep["margin"]))
    if rep["ok"]:
        head = min(rep["min"] - rep["lo"], rep["hi"] - rep["max"])
        lines.append(
            "    %d waypoints in %d pass(es), all inside the guarded window "
            "with %.1f mm to spare at the tightest"
            % (rep["n"], rep["n_passes"], 1e3*head))
        return lines
    lines.append(
        "    %d of %d waypoints are outside it, in %d of %d pass(es): %s"
        % (rep["n_out"], rep["n"], len(rep["passes_out"]), rep["n_passes"],
           ", ".join(str(k + 1) for k in rep["passes_out"][:12])
           + (" ..." if len(rep["passes_out"]) > 12 else "")))
    if rep["n_missing"]:
        lines.append(
            "    %d of them have NO surface within %.0f mm of the reference "
            "distance at all -- the sensor is aimed past the edge of the "
            "surface, or across a hole in it, and will measure nothing there"
            % (rep["n_missing"], 1e3*KEYENCE_RAY_SEARCH_M))
    x, y, z = rep["worst_xyz"]
    side = rep["worst_side"]
    if side == "missing":
        lines.append(
            "    worst: pass %d, waypoint %d, sensor at (%.3f, %.3f, %.3f) "
            "aimed at nothing"
            % (rep["worst_pass"] + 1, rep["worst_index"], x, y, z))
    else:
        limit = rep["lo"] if side == "near" else rep["hi"]
        hard = rep["hard_lo"] if side == "near" else rep["hard_hi"]
        lines.append(
            "    worst: %s at pass %d, waypoint %d, sensor at "
            "(%.3f, %.3f, %.3f) -- %.1f mm past the %s guard limit of %.0f mm "
            "and %.1f mm %s the sensor's own %.0f mm %s limit"
            % (_fmt_mm(rep["worst"]), rep["worst_pass"] + 1, rep["worst_index"],
               x, y, z, 1e3*abs(rep["worst"] - limit), side, 1e3*limit,
               1e3*abs(rep["worst"] - hard),
               "inside" if ((side == "near" and rep["worst"] >= hard)
                            or (side == "far" and rep["worst"] <= hard))
               else "OUTSIDE", 1e3*hard, side))
    lines.append(
        "    a profiler at the bottom of its range does not fail, it returns "
        "WRONG VALUES: flown at 58-61 mm over a surface-ground plate, "
        "26-39% of it came back scattered over 16 mm, and then got meshed.")
    lines.append(
        "    WHAT TO DO: the rough mesh this raster was planned over is the "
        "thing that is most likely wrong, not the sensor. A loose camera mount "
        "is enough to read a plate 13.6 mm low across the part and 22 mm low at "
        "one end. Re-seat and re-calibrate the camera and reconstruct the rough "
        "surface again, or plan this raster over a better surface -- a previous "
        "Keyence mesh, a CAD model. Do NOT raise KEYENCE_STANDOFF_MARGIN to get "
        "past this: the margin is what is left of the window, and spending it "
        "does not move the sensor.")
    return lines


#: How much of a raster may be DROPPED for being out of the measuring window
#: before the plan is refused outright instead.
#:
#: A handful of waypoints at the edge of the rough mesh is geometry, not a
#: fault: a Noether raster over any mesh with a boundary runs off it at the
#: ends, and a sensor aimed past the edge measures nothing there. A quarter of
#: the path out of window is a different statement entirely -- it says the mesh
#: the raster was planned over is wrong, and no amount of dropping fixes that.
#:
#: For scale: a raster over a cylinder in simulation puts 4 of 299 waypoints out
#: (1.3%), two of them aimed at nothing, against 295 sitting at exactly 80.0 mm;
#: a raster planned over a mesh 13.6-22 mm low was 26-39% out.
#:
#: IT APPLIES TO INTERIOR WAYPOINTS ONLY -- see out_of_window_shape(). A fraction
#: of the whole path is the wrong denominator for pass-END waypoints, because the
#: number of pass ends scales with the number of PASSES and not with path length.
#: Two rasters over the same part, minutes apart, show it:
#:
#:   1790 waypoints, 19 passes   2 out (0.11%)  ->  dropped, ran, meshed
#:     39 waypoints,  3 passes   3 out (7.69%)  ->  REFUSED
#:
#: Both are the same geometric fact -- a Noether raster runs off the end of a
#: bounded mesh and the sensor aimed past that edge measures nothing. The second
#: is a 24 x 66 mm ROI, which Noether plans as 3 passes of 13 poses, so ONE end
#: waypoint per pass is 7.7% of the path before anything is wrong. Capping the
#: whole path at 5% therefore refuses small regions for being small, which is
#: exactly backwards: a small ROI is what an operator draws when they want one
#: feature scanned in the hour the plan allows.
global KEYENCE_STANDOFF_DROP_FRACTION
KEYENCE_STANDOFF_DROP_FRACTION = 0.05

#: How much of ONE PASS may be trimmed off its ends before the trim stops being
#: an overrun and becomes evidence about the surface.
#:
#: Trim needs its own limit and it cannot be a fraction of the path -- that is
#: what the note above is about -- but it cannot be unlimited either. A raster
#: overruns a mesh boundary by about one waypoint spacing, so a pass losing one
#: or two waypoints off an end is geometry. A pass losing a THIRD of its length
#: off its ends is not: the surface it was planned over is at the wrong height
#: near the edges, which is precisely the failure this file guards against -- a
#: rough mesh 13.6 mm low across a plate and 22 mm low at ONE END puts the error
#: exactly where a per-path fraction would call it trim.
#:
#: Sized against the four cases on record, which is why it is 0.20 and not a
#: round guess:
#:
#:   19 passes of ~94,  2 out       ~1% of a pass                     -> fly
#:    3 passes of  13,  3 out      7.7% of a pass                     -> fly
#:   17 passes,        36 out, 4 passes lost WHOLE                    -> refuse
#:    7 passes of   9, 21 out       33% of a pass (edge overrun)      -> refuse
#:
#: A pass that is out from end to end is 100% and fails this by construction,
#: which is the behaviour wanted: it is not a shortened pass, it is no pass.
global KEYENCE_STANDOFF_TRIM_PASS_FRACTION
KEYENCE_STANDOFF_TRIM_PASS_FRACTION = 0.20


def out_of_window_shape(path, out):
    """Split out-of-window waypoints into pass-END TRIM and INTERIOR holes.

    THE TWO ARE DIFFERENT STATEMENTS ABOUT THE PLAN, which is why they are
    counted separately and held to different rules:

      TRIM      a contiguous run of out-of-window waypoints that TOUCHES the
                start or the end of a pass. This is the geometry of a finite
                surface: the raster overruns the mesh boundary, the sensor is
                aimed past the edge, and shortening the pass is the whole of the
                fix. It says nothing about whether the mesh is right.

      INTERIOR  out of window with in-window waypoints on BOTH sides. The
                surface is there and the plan is at the wrong height over it.
                That is what a wrong rough mesh looks like -- a mesh 13.6 mm low
                under a raster that came back 26-39% scattered -- and no amount
                of dropping fixes it.

    A pass that is out of window from end to end is NEITHER of those. There is no
    in-window waypoint for anything to be "between", so calling it an interior
    hole is wrong; and there is nothing left of the pass after trimming it, so
    calling it trim would let a raster that cannot be flown at all be reported as
    a shortened one. It is counted separately, as a pass lost whole.

    Returns (n_trim, n_interior, n_whole, passes), where `passes` is one dict per
    segment carrying its own counts -- so the report can name which pass has a
    hole in the middle rather than only how many waypoints are out.
    """
    out = np.asarray(out, dtype=bool).reshape(-1)
    n_trim = 0
    n_interior = 0
    n_whole = 0
    passes = []
    for k, (a, b) in enumerate(path.segments):
        seg = out[a:b]
        n = len(seg)
        trim = 0
        interior = 0
        whole = False
        if n and seg.all():
            whole = True
            n_whole += 1
        elif n:
            lead = 0
            while lead < n and seg[lead]:
                lead += 1
            tail = 0
            while tail < n and seg[n - 1 - tail]:
                tail += 1
            trim = lead + tail
            interior = int(seg.sum()) - trim
        n_trim += trim
        n_interior += interior
        passes.append({"index": k, "n": n, "n_trim": int(trim),
                       "n_interior": int(interior), "whole": whole,
                       # What share of THIS pass is out, which is the quantity
                       # the trim limit is on. See
                       # KEYENCE_STANDOFF_TRIM_PASS_FRACTION.
                       "share": (float(int(seg.sum()))/n) if n else 1.0})
    return int(n_trim), int(n_interior), int(n_whole), passes


def keyence_standoff_gate(path, surface, margin=None, allow=None,
                          what="keyence sweep", quiet=False, drop=None,
                          min_points=4, **kwargs):
    """Measure the plan's standoff, say it, and REFUSE it if it is out.

    REFUSED, NOT PRINTED. A warning emitted while the wrong thing proceeds is a
    warning nobody acts on: AzureReconstruction._report_standoff() is the
    precedent, and sweeps flew with its paragraph on screen. The Keyence's
    version of that failure is worse, because a depth camera in its dead zone at
    least produces a mesh that looks wrong; a profiler at the bottom of its range
    produces one that looks right.

    allow=True (or KEYENCE_ALLOW_BAD_STANDOFF in a config) is the deliberate
    override. It flies it anyway and prints what to expect rather than falling
    silent, which is the same contract allow_dead_zone has.

    Returns the report. Raises ValueError when the plan is out and not allowed.
    """
    global last_standoff_report
    if surface is None:
        _standoff_say(
            "    WARNING: there is no surface to check the %s standoff "
            "against, so nothing knows whether the sensor will be in its "
            "measuring window. Pass surface= the mesh the raster was planned "
            "over." % (what,))
        return None
    rep = keyence_standoff_report(path, surface, margin=margin, quiet=quiet,
                                  **kwargs)
    last_standoff_report = rep
    lines = describe_standoff_report(rep, what=what)
    if rep["ok"]:
        for line in lines:
            _standoff_say(line)
        return rep
    if allow is None:
        allow = bool(globals().get("KEYENCE_ALLOW_BAD_STANDOFF", False))
    for line in lines:
        _standoff_say(line)
    if allow:
        _standoff_say(
            "    ** FLYING OUT OF THE MEASURING WINDOW ANYWAY "
            "(KEYENCE_ALLOW_BAD_STANDOFF): expect the %d out-of-window "
            "waypoints to return scattered values rather than nothing, and "
            "expect those values to be meshed." % (rep["n_out"],))
        return rep

    # DROP THE FEW, REFUSE THE MANY -- the same contract the tooling-clearance
    # gate has had all along, and for the same reason it gives: losing a few
    # waypoints is a measurement with a hole in it, which is a result. Flying
    # them is wrong data, which is not.
    #
    # Without the third option the pipeline cannot finish unattended on ANY part
    # whose rough mesh has an edge: a Noether raster runs off the end of any
    # bounded surface, and the sensor aimed past that edge measures nothing. On a
    # simulated cylinder that is four waypoints of 299, and refuse-or-fly-
    # everything throws away all ten passes for them.
    frac = (KEYENCE_STANDOFF_DROP_FRACTION if drop is None else float(drop))
    crop = globals().get("_crop_path_by_mask")

    # TRIM IS ALWAYS ALLOWED; ONLY INTERIOR HOLES ARE CAPPED. The fraction used
    # taken over every out-of-window waypoint refuses a plan for having few
    # waypoints rather than for being wrong -- see the note on
    # KEYENCE_STANDOFF_DROP_FRACTION for the two runs that settled it.
    n_trim, n_interior, n_whole, shape = out_of_window_shape(path, rep["out"])
    interior_share = (float(n_interior)/rep["n"]) if rep["n"] else 1.0
    share = (float(rep["n_out"])/rep["n"]) if rep["n"] else 1.0
    rep = dict(rep)
    rep.update({"n_trim": n_trim, "n_interior": n_interior,
                "n_whole_passes_out": n_whole,
                "interior_share": interior_share, "shape": shape})

    # A pass that loses too much of its own length is not an overrun. Held per
    # pass, because that is the unit an overrun belongs to.
    pass_frac = KEYENCE_STANDOFF_TRIM_PASS_FRACTION
    gutted = [p for p in shape if p["share"] > pass_frac]
    rep["gutted_passes"] = [p["index"] for p in gutted]

    keep = ~np.asarray(rep["out"], bool)
    cropped = None
    if (crop is not None and rep["n_out"] and keep.any()
            and interior_share <= frac and not gutted):
        try:
            cropped = crop(path, keep, what,
                           min_points, "outside the measuring window")
        except RuntimeError:
            # Nothing survived the crop -- every run left was shorter than
            # min_points. Not a shortened raster: there is no raster. Fall
            # through to the refusal, which says what was measured and why.
            cropped = None
    if cropped is not None:
        n_miss = int(rep["n_missing"])
        _standoff_say(
            "    DROPPED %d of %d waypoints (%.1f%%) for being outside the "
            "window -- %d of them aimed at nothing, which would have measured "
            "nothing anyway. %d of %d passes remain."
            % (rep["n_out"], rep["n"], 100.0*share, n_miss,
               len(cropped.segments), len(path.segments)))
        _standoff_say(
            "    %d of those are pass ENDS (the raster overrunning the edge of "
            "a bounded mesh, which every raster does -- the pass is shortened) "
            "and %d are INTERIOR, with surface on both sides. Interior holes "
            "are what say the planning mesh is at the wrong height, and they "
            "are what the %.0f%% limit is on: this plan is at %.1f%%."
            % (n_trim, n_interior, 100.0*frac, 100.0*interior_share))
        _standoff_say(
            "    This is a HOLE IN THE MEASUREMENT, not a clean run: those "
            "waypoints are not measured and the mesh will have gaps there.")
        rep.update({"ok": True, "dropped": int(rep["n_out"]),
                    "path": cropped, "passes_after": len(cropped.segments),
                    "passes_before": len(path.segments)})
        last_standoff_report = rep
        return rep

    # WHICH of the three refusals this is. They are different problems and the
    # operator's next move differs, so do not blur them into one sentence.
    inner = [p["index"] + 1 for p in shape if p["n_interior"]]
    if gutted:
        worst_pass = max(gutted, key=lambda p: p["share"])
        whole = [p["index"] + 1 for p in gutted if p["whole"]]
        why = ("\n    %d of %d pass(es) lose more than %.0f%% of their own "
               "length to the window, worst pass %d at %.0f%% (%d of %d "
               "waypoints)%s. A raster overruns the edge of a bounded mesh by "
               "about ONE waypoint, so losing a third of a pass is not an "
               "overrun -- it is the surface being at the wrong height near "
               "that edge, which is the failure this gate exists for: a rough "
               "mesh 13.6 mm low across a plate and 22 mm low at ONE END puts "
               "the error precisely where a whole-path fraction would have "
               "called it trim. That is above the %.0f%% this gate will "
               "drop and fly around. The mesh is the thing to fix, not the "
               "margin."
               % (len(gutted), rep["n_passes"], 100.0*pass_frac,
                  worst_pass["index"] + 1, 100.0*worst_pass["share"],
                  int(round(worst_pass["share"]*worst_pass["n"])),
                  worst_pass["n"],
                  ("" if not whole else
                   "; %d of them out from end to end (pass(es) %s)"
                   % (len(whole), ", ".join(str(k) for k in whole[:12])
                      + (" ..." if len(whole) > 12 else ""))),
                  100.0*frac))
    elif interior_share > frac:
        why = ("\n    %d of them are INTERIOR -- surface on both sides, so the "
               "plan is at the wrong height over ground that IS there -- which "
               "is %.1f%% of the raster against the %.0f%% this gate will drop "
               "and fly around. Pass(es) %s. The mesh is the thing to fix, not "
               "the margin."
               % (n_interior, 100.0*interior_share, 100.0*frac,
                  ", ".join(str(k) for k in inner[:12])
                  + (" ..." if len(inner) > 12 else "")))
    elif cropped is None and crop is not None:
        why = ("\n    NOTHING IS LEFT TO FLY: %d of %d pass(es) are out of the "
               "window from end to end, and no run of at least %d in-window "
               "waypoints survives anywhere. This is not a raster with a hole "
               "in it, it is a raster that cannot be flown -- the surface it "
               "was planned over is at the wrong height, not merely finite."
               % (n_whole, rep["n_passes"], min_points))
    else:
        why = ("\n    %.1f%% of the raster is out of the window and this gate "
               "has no path-cropping function available, so it cannot drop the "
               "few and fly the rest." % (100.0*share,))
    raise ValueError(
        "this %s is refused: %d of %d waypoints command a standoff outside "
        "%.0f..%.0f mm, worst %s at pass %d.\n%s"
        % (what, rep["n_out"], rep["n"], 1e3*rep["lo"], 1e3*rep["hi"],
           _fmt_mm(rep["worst"]), rep["worst_pass"] + 1,
           "\n".join(lines[1:]))
        + why)


def check_keyence_standoff(path=None, surface=None, rough=None, region=None,
                           margin=None, source=None, **kwargs):
    """WILL THE KEYENCE RASTER BE IN ITS WINDOW? Nothing moves.

    Run it before the recorder starts. It plans the raster THE SAME WAY THE RUN
    PLANS IT and prints the same analysis the gate prints, because it is the
    same call and the same gate -- check_rough_plan() in ECAutomatedScan.dgp is
    the precedent, and the reason is that a checker which is a second
    implementation of the thing it checks eventually disagrees with it. IK is
    randomised and meshes are noisy, so "eventually" is every run: a sweep
    checker built that way once reported 2 flyable directions where the run
    itself found 0 of 8 over the same region minutes later.

        check_keyence_standoff()                       plan it and grade it
        check_keyence_standoff(surface=0.9402)         ...against a touched-off
                                                       plate top instead
        check_keyence_standoff(surface="previous.ply") ...against a better mesh
        check_keyence_standoff(path=already_planned)   grade a path in hand

    The second and third forms are the interesting ones. Grading the plan
    against the mesh it was PLANNED over can only find inconsistencies inside
    the plan; grading it against a surface that came from somewhere else finds
    the mesh itself being wrong, which is the failure that matters.

    Returns the report. It does not raise -- it is a check, so the refusal is
    printed in full and reported as ok=False rather than thrown at somebody who
    asked a question.
    """
    if path is None:
        # THE SAME CALL THE RUN MAKES. reconstruct_fine() is
        # reconstruct_specimen(source="keyence", ...) with one setdefault, and
        # plan_only=True stops at the plan -- so plan_sweep() runs, and its own
        # gate runs inside it, over the same mesh with the same orientation and
        # the same clearance ladder.
        #
        # The setdefault is mirrored rather than shared because reconstruct_fine
        # lives in the config: if a config grows a second one, this drifts, and
        # the drift shows up as a different orient in the printed line below.
        if globals().get("KEYENCE_ORIENTATION") is not None:
            kwargs.setdefault("orient", KEYENCE_ORIENTATION)
        if rough is None:
            rough = globals().get("rough_mesh")
        if rough is None:
            raise RuntimeError(
                "the Keyence raster is a Noether toolpath over a rough mesh, "
                "and there is none. Run reconstruct_rough() first, or pass "
                "rough= a mesh on disk.")
        if region is None:
            region = globals().get("detected_region")
        try:
            path = reconstruct_specimen(source="keyence", rough_mesh=rough,
                                        region=region, name="specimen",
                                        plan_only=True, **kwargs)
        except ValueError as e:
            # plan_sweep()'s own gate refused it. That IS the answer; print it
            # where a check's output belongs rather than as a traceback.
            print("  REFUSED by the standoff guard while planning:\n%s" % (e,))
            return last_standoff_report
        if surface is None:
            surface = rough
    if surface is None:
        surface = rough if rough is not None else globals().get("rough_mesh")
    try:
        return keyence_standoff_gate(path, surface, margin=margin,
                                     source=source, what="keyence raster",
                                     quiet=False, **{
                                         k: v for k, v in kwargs.items()
                                         if k in ("tf_offset", "axis",
                                                  "ref_dist", "range_m",
                                                  "search")})
    except ValueError as e:
        print("  REFUSED: %s" % (e,))
        return last_standoff_report


# ==============================================================================
# LAYER 2 AND 3: WHAT THE SENSOR ACTUALLY RETURNS
# ==============================================================================
# The plan-time gate can only be as right as the surface it is handed. This half
# asks the sensor, which has no opinion about the rough mesh at all.

def keyence_profile_standoff(msg, ref_dist=None):
    """(m,) standoffs in metres from one /profiles PointCloud2, finite only.

    keyence_driver_node packs each column as (x_across, 0, z) in
    lj_v7080_optical_frame, where z is the device's own reading and POSITIVE z
    is TOWARDS THE HEAD -- so the distance from the head is

        standoff = keyence_ref_dist - z

    and the device's +/-23 mm of travel maps onto 0.057..0.103 m the right way
    round. Out-of-range columns are +inf (REP-117) and are dropped here; a
    profile of nothing returns an empty array, which is a fact about coverage
    and not about standoff.

    Reads only z. This runs on every message at the real head's 216 Hz with 800
    columns each, so it must not be the thing that is slow: the fields are
    located once from the message's own layout rather than assumed, and nothing
    is transformed into world -- the standoff IS a sensor-frame quantity, which
    is exactly why this check needs no TF, no pose and no clock.
    """
    ref = keyence_ref_dist(ref_dist)
    fields = {f.name: (int(f.offset), int(f.datatype)) for f in msg.fields}
    if "z" not in fields:
        return np.zeros(0)
    offset, datatype = fields["z"]
    dt = {7: "<f4", 8: "<f8"}.get(datatype)
    if dt is None:
        return np.zeros(0)
    step = int(msg.point_step)
    n = int(msg.width)*int(msg.height)
    if n <= 0 or step <= 0:
        return np.zeros(0)
    buf = np.frombuffer(bytes(msg.data), dtype=np.uint8)
    need = n*step
    if buf.size < need:
        n = buf.size//step
        if n <= 0:
            return np.zeros(0)
        need = n*step
    z = np.frombuffer(buf[:need].tobytes(), dtype=np.dtype({
        "names": ["z"], "formats": [dt], "offsets": [offset],
        "itemsize": step}), count=n)["z"].astype(np.float64)
    z = z[np.isfinite(z)]
    return ref - z


def keyence_range_report(standoff, commanded=None, margin=None, range_m=None,
                         n_profiles=0, n_empty=0, warn_fraction=None):
    """What the sensor is measuring, against the window and against the plan.

    `standoff` is metres from the head, as keyence_profile_standoff() returns
    it. `commanded` is what the plan asked for at the same place, if the caller
    knows -- and the difference between the two is the error in the surface the
    plan was made from, measured with no touch-off and no calibration.

      bias = commanded - measured

    positive meaning the real surface is NEARER the head than the plan believed.
    """
    lo, hi, hard_lo, hard_hi = keyence_window(margin=margin, range_m=range_m)
    warn = float(KEYENCE_STANDOFF_WARN_FRACTION if warn_fraction is None
                 else warn_fraction)
    d = np.asarray(standoff, dtype=np.float64).reshape(-1)
    d = d[np.isfinite(d)]
    rep = {"n": int(d.size), "lo": lo, "hi": hi, "hard_lo": hard_lo,
           "hard_hi": hard_hi, "margin": keyence_standoff_margin(margin),
           "n_profiles": int(n_profiles), "n_empty": int(n_empty),
           "warn_fraction": warn, "commanded": None, "bias": None,
           "bias_limit": float(globals().get("KEYENCE_MESH_BIAS_LIMIT",
                                             KEYENCE_MESH_BIAS_LIMIT_M))}
    if d.size == 0:
        rep.update({"min": float("nan"), "max": float("nan"),
                    "median": float("nan"), "p5": float("nan"),
                    "p95": float("nan"), "spread": float("nan"),
                    "near_fraction": 0.0, "far_fraction": 0.0,
                    "tight_fraction": 0.0, "out_fraction": 0.0,
                    "ok": True, "empty": True, "why": []})
        return rep
    near = float((d < lo).mean())
    far = float((d > hi).mean())
    p5, p95 = float(np.percentile(d, 5)), float(np.percentile(d, 95))
    rep.update({
        "min": float(d.min()), "max": float(d.max()),
        "median": float(np.median(d)), "p5": p5, "p95": p95,
        "spread": p95 - p5,
        "near_fraction": near, "far_fraction": far,
        "tight_fraction": near + far,
        "out_fraction": float(((d < hard_lo) | (d > hard_hi)).mean()),
        "empty": False,
    })
    # Each reason carries a KIND beside it, so a caller can suppress one it has
    # already printed without matching on the text -- over a 17-row raster a
    # uniform mesh error is one fact, not seventeen, and a paragraph repeated
    # seventeen times is how a real finding becomes noise.
    why, kinds = [], []
    if near >= warn:
        kinds.append("near")
        why.append("%.1f%% of these points are within %.0f mm of the NEAR "
                   "limit (%.0f mm), where a triangulation profiler stops "
                   "returning the surface and starts returning noise"
                   % (100.0*near, 1e3*rep["margin"], 1e3*hard_lo))
    if far >= warn:
        kinds.append("far")
        why.append("%.1f%% of these points are within %.0f mm of the FAR "
                   "limit (%.0f mm)" % (100.0*far, 1e3*rep["margin"], 1e3*hard_hi))
    if commanded is not None and np.isfinite(commanded):
        bias = float(commanded) - float(np.median(d))
        rep["commanded"] = float(commanded)
        rep["bias"] = bias
        # A MEDIAN IS THE RIGHT SUMMARY ONLY WHERE THE SURFACE IS FLAT ACROSS THE
        # LINE, and the laser line is 31 mm wide.
        #
        # The plan commands a standoff at ONE POINT -- the middle of the line. On
        # a surface that curves across the line the sensor returns a RANGE, and
        # the commanded point sits at one end of it, not in the middle: on a
        # convex crest the middle of the line is the NEAREST return. Comparing the
        # commanded standoff with the median then reads the sagitta as a mesh
        # error.
        #
        # Measured on a 131 x 65 x 67 mm cylinder: comparing against the median
        # refuses the sweep with "commanded 80.0, measuring 62.8, so the surface
        # is 17.2 mm BELOW the real one" while the planning mesh's crest is
        # accurate to 0.4 mm. The mesh is right and the inference is wrong.
        #
        # So when the line spans more than a flat surface would, ask the weaker
        # question that is still sound: is the commanded standoff somewhere in
        # what the sensor actually sees? If it is, the plan agrees with the sensor
        # about where the surface is and the spread is the part's own shape. If it
        # is not, the disagreement is real and survives the curvature.
        rep["line_spread"] = float(rep["spread"])
        flat_line = float(globals().get("KEYENCE_LINE_FLAT_SPREAD_M",
                                        KEYENCE_LINE_FLAT_SPREAD_M))
        curved = rep["spread"] > flat_line
        in_range = (rep["min"] - rep["margin"] <= float(commanded)
                    <= rep["max"] + rep["margin"])
        rep["bias_from_curvature"] = bool(curved and in_range)
        if curved and in_range:
            rep["bias_note"] = (
                "the laser line spans %.1f mm of height here (p5..p95), which is "
                "a CURVED surface across the line rather than a mesh error: the "
                "commanded %s lies inside the %.1f..%.1f mm the sensor actually "
                "returns. The %.1f mm 'bias' is the sagitta, and the median is "
                "not the standoff at the point the plan commanded."
                % (1e3*rep["spread"], _fmt_mm(commanded), 1e3*rep["min"],
                   1e3*rep["max"], 1e3*abs(bias)))
        if abs(bias) > rep["bias_limit"] and not rep["bias_from_curvature"]:
            # WHICH WAY ROUND, and it is worth being slow about. bias is
            # commanded minus measured, so a POSITIVE bias means the real
            # surface is NEARER the head than the plan believed -- for a sensor
            # looking down, that is a real surface HIGHER than the planning
            # surface, which is to say a planning surface that reads LOW: a
            # bias of +13.6 mm is a planning mesh 13.6 mm below the part.
            kinds.append("bias")
            why.append(
                "the plan commanded %s here and the sensor is measuring %s, so "
                "the surface this raster was planned over is %.1f mm %s the "
                "real one -- over the %.0f mm this pipeline will trust"
                % (_fmt_mm(commanded), _fmt_mm(np.median(d)), 1e3*abs(bias),
                   "BELOW" if bias > 0 else "ABOVE", 1e3*rep["bias_limit"]))
    rep["why"] = why
    rep["why_kinds"] = kinds
    rep["ok"] = not why
    return rep


def describe_range_report(rep, what="this pass", skip=()):
    """One summary line, plus a line per reason it is not ok.

    `skip` drops reasons by kind ("near", "far", "bias"), which is how the
    monitor says a uniform mesh error once instead of once per row. The report
    itself is unchanged -- what is suppressed is the repetition, not the fact.
    """
    if rep.get("empty"):
        return ["    %s: no finite returns at all (%d profile(s), %d empty)"
                % (what, rep["n_profiles"], rep["n_empty"])]
    lines = ["    %s: %d points, standoff %.1f..%.1f mm (median %.1f, "
             "p5..p95 spread %.2f mm) against the %.0f..%.0f mm window"
             % (what, rep["n"], 1e3*rep["min"], 1e3*rep["max"],
                1e3*rep["median"], 1e3*rep["spread"],
                1e3*rep["hard_lo"], 1e3*rep["hard_hi"])]
    if rep.get("bias_note") and "bias" not in skip:
        lines.append("    ** %s" % (rep["bias_note"],))
    if rep.get("bias") is not None:
        lines[0] += ("; commanded %.1f mm, so the planning surface is %.1f mm "
                     "%s" % (1e3*rep["commanded"], 1e3*abs(rep["bias"]),
                             "high" if rep["bias"] < 0 else "low"))
    kinds = rep.get("why_kinds") or [None]*len(rep.get("why", []))
    for kind, reason in zip(kinds, rep.get("why", [])):
        if kind in skip:
            continue
        lines.append("    ** %s" % (reason,))
    return lines


def keyence_range_gate(standoff, commanded=None, margin=None, allow=None,
                       what="the first pose of the keyence sweep", **kwargs):
    """The pre-flight gate: what the sensor sees, before anything flies.

    Called from _KeyenceReconstruction.profile_facts(), which runs after the arm
    has reached the sweep's first pose and before a row is flown. Everything it
    needs is already there -- a profile from the sensor and the standoff the
    plan commanded at that waypoint -- and the whole check costs the three
    seconds profile_topic_facts() was already spending.

    REFUSES when the measurement is outside the guarded window, and when it
    disagrees with the plan by more than KEYENCE_MESH_BIAS_LIMIT_M. The second
    is the one that catches a raster planned over a mesh at the wrong height,
    and it has to be the second and not the first because 66 mm IS inside the
    window: such a run is refusable not because the sensor is out of range at
    the first pose but because the surface it was planned over is 13.6 mm wrong,
    and a surface that wrong globally is wrong by more than the remaining 4.4 mm
    somewhere.

    Ten minutes of flying, and then a mesh that looks like a mesh. Three seconds
    here.
    """
    global last_range_report
    rep = keyence_range_report(standoff, commanded=commanded, margin=margin,
                               **kwargs)
    last_range_report = rep
    for line in describe_range_report(rep, what=what):
        _standoff_say(line)
    if rep["ok"]:
        return rep
    if allow is None:
        allow = bool(globals().get("KEYENCE_ALLOW_BAD_STANDOFF", False))
    fix = ("Re-seat and re-calibrate the camera and reconstruct the rough "
           "surface again, or plan this raster over a better surface. The "
           "sensor is telling you where the part is; the mesh is the thing "
           "that is wrong.")
    if allow:
        _standoff_say("    ** SWEEPING ANYWAY (KEYENCE_ALLOW_BAD_STANDOFF). %s"
                      % (fix,))
        return rep
    raise RuntimeError(
        "this keyence sweep is refused before it flies: %s\n  %s"
        % ("; ".join(rep["why"]), fix))


class KeyenceStandoffMonitor(object):
    """Watches /profiles during the sweep and says where in the window it is.

    THE LAYER THAT SEES A LOCAL ERROR. The plan-time gate grades the plan
    against a surface, and when the only surface available is the wrong one it
    has nothing to find. The pre-flight gate asks the sensor once, at the first
    pose. This asks it continuously, so it is the only thing in the pipeline that
    can say, while it is happening, that the last few passes of a part are being
    measured at the bottom of the range: 41.7% of the worst pass's points within
    5 mm of the near limit, against 0.0% on every good pass, and a p5..p95 spread
    of 19 mm against 0.2 mm.

    It reports through progress.dpi, which is where the rest of a long stage's
    output goes and which reaches the operator from a worker thread.

    Cheap by construction: one subscription, z only, no transform, no TF lookup,
    no pose. The whole per-message cost is a strided read of 800 float32 and a
    handful of comparisons -- measured at well under a millisecond, against the
    real head's 216 Hz.

    The pure-numpy half (add_standoff / start_pass / end_pass / summary) has no
    ROS in it at all, which is what lets keyence_standoff_test.py drive the
    whole thing from a recorded point cloud.
    """

    #: How many profiles the whole-sweep buffer keeps before it starts thinning.
    #: The real head runs at 216.8 Hz with 800 columns, so a ten-minute sweep is
    #: 130 000 profiles and 830 MB of float64 if every one is kept -- and this
    #: pipeline has been OOM-killed by a multi-gigabyte buffer before (the
    #: clearance gate). Past the cap every second profile is dropped and the
    #: stride doubles again at the next one, which keeps the buffer bounded and
    #: the DISTRIBUTION unbiased: what the summary reports is percentiles and
    #: fractions, and a uniform subsample preserves both. Per-pass buffers are
    #: not thinned -- they are freed at end_pass() and a pass is a minute.
    MAX_KEPT_PROFILES = 4000

    def __init__(self, topic=None, commanded=None, margin=None, ref_dist=None,
                 warn_fraction=None, path=None):
        self.topic = topic or globals().get("KEYENCE_MONITOR_TOPIC") \
            or KEYENCE_MONITOR_TOPIC
        self.margin = margin
        self.ref_dist = ref_dist
        self.warn_fraction = warn_fraction
        # (n,) commanded standoff per waypoint, so a pass can be compared
        # against what it was told to do. None is fine -- the window check does
        # not need it, only the bias does.
        self.commanded = (None if commanded is None else
                          np.asarray(commanded, dtype=np.float64).reshape(-1))
        self.path = path
        self._lock = threading.RLock()
        self._pass = None
        self._all = []
        self._all_profiles = 0
        self._all_empty = 0
        self._stride = 1
        self._node = None
        self._thread = None
        self._stop = None
        self._bias_said = False
        self.reports = []
        self.loud_passes = []

    # -- the numbers -----------------------------------------------------------

    def add_standoff(self, d):
        """One profile's worth of standoffs. Thread safe, and the fast path."""
        d = np.asarray(d, dtype=np.float64).reshape(-1)
        with self._lock:
            self._all_profiles += 1
            if d.size == 0:
                self._all_empty += 1
            elif self._all_profiles % self._stride == 0:
                self._all.append(d)
                if len(self._all) > self.MAX_KEPT_PROFILES:
                    # Halve what is held and double the stride, so the buffer
                    # stays bounded and the sample stays uniform over the sweep.
                    del self._all[1::2]
                    self._stride *= 2
            if self._pass is not None:
                self._pass["profiles"] += 1
                if d.size == 0:
                    self._pass["empty"] += 1
                else:
                    self._pass["data"].append(d)

    def start_pass(self, index, total=None, commanded=None):
        """Begin accumulating for one raster pass."""
        with self._lock:
            if commanded is None and self.commanded is not None \
                    and self.path is not None:
                segs = self.path.segments
                if 0 <= index < len(segs):
                    a, b = segs[index]
                    seg = self.commanded[a:b]
                    seg = seg[np.isfinite(seg)]
                    commanded = float(np.median(seg)) if seg.size else None
            self._pass = {"index": int(index), "total": total,
                          "commanded": commanded, "data": [], "profiles": 0,
                          "empty": 0}

    def end_pass(self, quiet=False):
        """Close the pass, report it, and say so loudly if it was at a limit."""
        with self._lock:
            cur = self._pass
            self._pass = None
        if cur is None:
            return None
        d = (np.concatenate(cur["data"]) if cur["data"] else np.zeros(0))
        rep = keyence_range_report(d, commanded=cur["commanded"],
                                   margin=self.margin,
                                   n_profiles=cur["profiles"],
                                   n_empty=cur["empty"],
                                   warn_fraction=self.warn_fraction)
        rep["pass"] = cur["index"]
        self.reports.append(rep)
        if not rep["ok"]:
            self.loud_passes.append(cur["index"])
        if not quiet:
            what = ("sweep row %d%s standoff"
                    % (cur["index"] + 1,
                       "/%d" % cur["total"] if cur["total"] else ""))
            # A UNIFORM MESH ERROR IS ONE FACT. The bias is the same number on
            # every row of a raster planned over a surface that is globally
            # wrong, so it is shouted once and then left in the per-row summary
            # line, where the number still appears. The limit reasons are NOT
            # suppressed: those are local, and which rows have them is the
            # whole point of reporting per row.
            skip = ("bias",) if self._bias_said else ()
            if "bias" in (rep.get("why_kinds") or ()):
                self._bias_said = True
            for line in describe_range_report(rep, what=what, skip=skip):
                _standoff_say(line)
        return rep

    def summary(self, quiet=False):
        """The whole sweep, and an unmissable block when it went wrong.

        Printed at stop(), which is before the minutes of meshing rather than
        after them: the operator is told the capture was bad while there is
        still something to do about it other than look at the result.
        """
        with self._lock:
            d = (np.concatenate(self._all) if self._all else np.zeros(0))
            profiles, empty, stride = (self._all_profiles, self._all_empty,
                                       self._stride)
        rep = keyence_range_report(d, margin=self.margin, n_profiles=profiles,
                                   n_empty=empty,
                                   warn_fraction=self.warn_fraction)
        rep["loud_passes"] = list(self.loud_passes)
        rep["n_passes"] = len(self.reports)
        rep["stride"] = stride
        if quiet:
            return rep
        what = ("the whole sweep" if stride == 1 else
                "the whole sweep (every %dth of %d profiles; see "
                "MAX_KEPT_PROFILES)" % (stride, profiles))
        for line in describe_range_report(rep, what=what):
            _standoff_say(line)
        if self.loud_passes:
            _standoff_say(
                "    ** %d of %d pass(es) measured at the edge of the "
                "profiler's window: %s. The points those passes contributed "
                "are not measurements of the part -- at the bottom of its range "
                "this sensor returns scattered values rather than nothing, and "
                "the mesh about to be built will contain them. On a flat plate "
                "that has been 26-39%% of the scan, with nothing downstream "
                "noticing."
                % (len(self.loud_passes), rep["n_passes"],
                   ", ".join("row %d" % (k + 1) for k in self.loud_passes[:12])
                   + (" ..." if len(self.loud_passes) > 12 else "")))
            _standoff_say(
                "    the fix is upstream of the sensor: the surface this "
                "raster was planned over put it there. Re-calibrate the camera "
                "and reconstruct the rough surface again, or re-plan over a "
                "better one, and run check_keyence_standoff() before flying.")
        return rep

    # -- the subscription ------------------------------------------------------

    def start(self):
        """Subscribe and spin on our own thread. Never fatal.

        A monitor that takes down the sweep it is watching would be a bad trade,
        so every failure here degrades to "no monitoring" with a line saying so.
        The sweep is still worth flying without it.
        """
        try:
            import rclpy
            from rclpy.node import Node
            from sensor_msgs.msg import PointCloud2
        except Exception as e:
            _standoff_say("    no standoff monitoring on %s (%s)"
                          % (self.topic, e))
            return self
        try:
            self._node = Node("keyence_standoff_monitor")
            self._node.create_subscription(PointCloud2, self.topic,
                                           self._got, 10)
        except Exception as e:
            _standoff_say("    no standoff monitoring on %s (%s)"
                          % (self.topic, e))
            self._node = None
            return self
        self._stop = threading.Event()

        def _spin():
            try:
                from dataguzzler_python.context import InitFreeThread
                InitFreeThread()
            except Exception:
                pass
            while not self._stop.is_set():
                try:
                    rclpy.spin_once(self._node, timeout_sec=0.1)
                except Exception:
                    break

        self._thread = threading.Thread(target=_spin,
                                        name="keyence_standoff_monitor")
        self._thread.daemon = True
        self._thread.start()
        return self

    def _got(self, msg):
        try:
            self.add_standoff(keyence_profile_standoff(msg,
                                                       ref_dist=self.ref_dist))
        except Exception:
            # One malformed message must not stop the watch: a monitor that
            # stops counting looks exactly like a sweep with nothing wrong.
            pass

    def stop(self, quiet=False):
        """Stop watching and return the summary."""
        if self._stop is not None:
            self._stop.set()
        if self._thread is not None:
            self._thread.join(timeout=2.0)
        if self._node is not None:
            try:
                self._node.destroy_node()
            except Exception:
                pass
        self._node = self._thread = self._stop = None
        return self.summary(quiet=quiet)
